{"kind":"report","text":"CLOSE 6ef29521. Your conditions say a well-argued close is a satisfactory outcome, and this is one. The decisive fact is not in the diff; it is in the log, and it is yours.\n\nTHE HEAD'S AUTHORIZATION WAS ALREADY WITHDRAWN, BY YOU, NAMING THIS HEAD. Its only content commit 2d650849 carries Rests-On for request 51da7efc, #2817. That request is retired by supersede act 28825910, actor planner, effective, on 2026-08-19. The withdrawal text says: overtaken, the README was rewritten directly on main by the operator after this head was reported, the head was never reviewed and is not landable, and a further README pass deserves a fresh request on the current text. You filed b4e95589 on 2026-08-22, three and a half days later. I read both timestamps out of the projection rather than inferring them.\n\nSo 'revive the dormant head' is not an available action. Any artifact standing on that chain is stale from birth and unmergeable. The dormant-branch sweep rediscovered a branch whose authorization you had already retired with a written ruling. This report is not asking you to re-decide; it is asking you to reconcile b4e95589 with 28825910, and your earlier ruling is the one I would keep.\n\nTHE PROBLEM IT NAMED IS MOSTLY GONE. Of #2817's defects, the two correctness complaints -- the document announcing its own wrongness, and 'every artifact' claimed as automatic coverage -- have no match anywhere in README.md, docs/why.md, docs/README.md or docs/concepts/staleness.md at main aa4f8756. What survives is an ordering preference, which is taste rather than defect.\n\nWHAT REVIVING WOULD REINTRODUCE, three things I checked at the file rather than taking on report. The branch says vulnerability reports go through GitHub's private advisory channel; main says report privately and directly to the maintainer, never a public issue or workroom -- wording changed deliberately after main went red on the public-surface test, through two review lanes and an explicit decision by Hugh. The branch has no trust-boundary paragraph at all: grep for 'trusted processes only' returns zero, and main carries it at README.md:71 because the acknowledge-trusted-processes flag was removed and the posture had to live in the docs instead. Losing that is a security regression. It also restores the old video link, which main replaced today, and points at workroom #2816 as forthcoming when #2816 is itself retired and gs artifacts has since shipped with its own reference page and MCP twin.\n\nTHE DESIGN, WHICH YOU SAID YOU HAD NOT AUDITED. Two objections. It sells one application's projection as the platform's identity -- artifact currentness is a reading of the workroom fold, and main correctly opens on a substrate whose workroom is its first application; the branch's own correction, that the workroom is one reading and not a universal ontology, arrives at line 190 of 259. And its headline demo advertises the absence of the feature it leads with: a third of the README, in position two, is fifty-six lines of shell containing a twenty-five-line jq program that hand-joins two projections. That was arguable in August. It is not now: gs artifacts --path prints the currentness line directly, and gs status --all renders an artifact table. The twelve days obsoleted precisely the part the head is built around. It would also compress roughly a hundred and thirty lines of accurate Kernel, Nexus, Folds, schema and checkpoint material into an eighteen-line summary, which is a bad trade for the jq script in a README that agents read.\n\nONE TEST I WOULD REJECT REGARDLESS. TestREADMELeadsWithTheTrackedArtifactOutcome pins the README's first two sentences byte for byte, em-dash and line wrap included. Every other docset test gates a structural or factual property -- citations resolve, examples run, the surface covers every subcommand, retiring an act flares exactly its pages. None pins prose. README.md has around twenty commits in twelve days; this would make every wording edit a red gate, and its banned-phrase list includes the bare string 'every artifact', which is an ordinary phrase.\n\nWHAT IS WORTH SALVAGING, and it is small and cheap because it auto-merges clean. The docs/why.md three-hunk de-overclaim -- every document becomes many documents, documents-name-the-acts becomes tracked-artifacts-name-the-acts with the re-check-not-wrong gloss, and the paragraph saying gitseq does not discover causal edges from files, imports or prose. The matching one-hunk change in docs/README.md. And TestREADMERelativeLinksResolve, because README.md is not gated by docset at all today: DocsDir is 'docs' and the walker never sees the README, so nothing checks that its links resolve or its code runs. I verified the branch's example still runs clean against a gs built from current main, exit 0 with all four assertions passing, and that all eleven of its relative links resolve -- twelve days with no drift, which is why the salvage is worth taking rather than rewriting.\n\nCOST, IF YOU DISAGREE AND WANT IT ANYWAY. README.md conflicts wholesale: merge-tree gives a content conflict, both sides having rewritten from the same base, so it is a recut and not a rebase. README.md also carries three live artifacts by three different actors right now -- mine, yours and codex's -- so any README merge needs all three named in the verdict and two are not mine to retire. Plus a fresh request, since the old one is retired, and a fresh review lane.\n\nI HAVE NOT DELETED THE BRANCH REF. Your conditions say retire the branch, and I am reading that as retiring the work rather than the ref: the evidence for this close lives inside 6ef29521, and deleting the ref would make this report uncheckable. Say the word if you want the ref gone and I will delete it after you have ratified."}